1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,197
A British bomber dropped a wooden box by parachute
over occupied France in August 1941.

2
00:00:07,237 --> 00:00:11,085
It was meant for one prisoner,
Douglas Bader.

3
00:00:11,125 --> 00:00:15,075
Ten years earlier,
Bader had lost both legs in a crash,

4
00:00:15,115 --> 00:00:17,109
but he flew again
on artificial legs.

5
00:00:17,149 --> 00:00:20,787
Earlier that August, his Spitfire
was shot down over France.

6
00:00:20,827 --> 00:00:25,762
As he bailed out, his right artificial leg
got stuck in the cockpit and was torn off.

7
00:00:25,802 --> 00:00:29,762
The German fighter ace Adolf Galland
invited him to his airfield

8
00:00:29,802 --> 00:00:32,485
and let him sit
in his own Messerschmitt.

9
00:00:32,525 --> 00:00:36,360
The Germans found the lost leg
in the wreck and repaired it.

10
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,570
Bader used it to escape.

11
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:42,700
He climbed out of his hospital window
on knotted bedsheets,

12
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:44,940
but was caught the next day.

13
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:50,415
Galland sent word to England: send one plane
with a spare leg, and it will not be shot at.

14
00:00:50,455 --> 00:00:53,465
The British did not want
a favor from the enemy.

15
00:00:53,505 --> 00:00:56,968
One of their bombers
dropped the box during a raid.

16
00:00:57,008 --> 00:00:59,198
Inside was a new leg for Bader.

17
00:00:59,238 --> 00:01:02,428
With two legs again,
he kept trying to escape,

18
00:01:02,468 --> 00:01:06,256
so often that the Germans threatened
to take his legs away at night.

19
00:01:06,296 --> 00:01:08,729
In the end they locked him up in Colditz.

20
00:01:08,769 --> 00:01:13,189
American troops freed him
in April 1945.

21
00:01:13,229 --> 00:01:18,959
His wife Thelma was waiting at home, and in June
he led the victory flypast over London.

22
00:01:18,999 --> 00:01:23,189
And Adolf Galland, the enemy
who had asked for that leg,

23
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:26,279
remained his friend
for more than forty years.
